deeper_reasoning_2:
\textbf{Claim.}
For the shifted monopole image chain, the exponential center-vortex localization follows directly from the period $NL_3$ and the absence of a zero Fourier charge.  More precisely, if $r:=|x|$, then
\[
\vec\sigma(x,x_3)=O\left(\exp\left[-\frac{2\pi r}{NL_3}\right]\right)
\qquad (r\to\infty).
\]
Thus the transverse localization scale is $NL_3/(2\pi)$.

\textbf{Premises.}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{(P1)} & N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge2},\quad L_3>0,\quad P:=NL_3,\quad r:=|x|.\\[1mm]
\text{(P2)} & \displaystyle \Psi:=\frac{g^2}{4\pi iL_4}\vec\sigma
\text{ is the shifted image-chain solution and }\Psi\to0\text{ as }r\to\infty.\\[2mm]
\text{(P3)} & \displaystyle \Psi\text{ is }P\text{-periodic on the covering coordinate }x_3
\text{ after collecting one complete }N\text{-image block}.\\[2mm]
\text{(P4)} & \displaystyle \sum_{l=0}^{N-1}\vec\nu_{n-l}=0.\\[2mm]
\text{(P5)} & \displaystyle \text{A }P\text{-periodic source with Fourier coefficient }s\in\mathbb{Z}
\text{ has longitudinal momentum }p_s=\frac{2\pi s}{P}.\\[3mm]
\text{(P6)} & \displaystyle \text{For }s\ne0\text{, the transverse Green tail of a two-dimensional mode with }
|p_s|\text{ is }O(e^{-|p_s|r}).\\[2mm]
\text{(P7)} & \displaystyle \text{The zero Fourier coefficient is the total charge in one }P\text{-period block}.
\end{array}
\]

\textbf{Derivation.}
Because the shifted construction repeats after $N$ shifts by $L_3$, the natural period of the collected image chain is
\begin{align}
P&=NL_3 .
\end{align}
Within one such period, the coefficient of the zero Fourier mode is proportional to the total block charge.  By (P4) and (P7),
\begin{align}
\widehat Q_0&\propto\sum_{l=0}^{N-1}\vec\nu_{n-l}=0 .
\end{align}
Hence the long-distance field has no $s=0$ term.  This is the decisive point: a nonzero zero mode would have produced a two-dimensional Coulomb tail, while the shifted neutral block removes that channel.

All remaining Fourier channels have $s\ne0$.  By (P5),
\begin{align}
|p_s|&=\frac{2\pi |s|}{P}
      =\frac{2\pi |s|}{NL_3},
      \qquad s\in\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0\}.
\end{align}
Therefore the smallest possible nonzero momentum is
\begin{align}
p_{\min}
&=\min_{s\in\mathbb{Z}\setminus\{0\}}\frac{2\pi |s|}{NL_3}\\
&=\frac{2\pi}{NL_3}.
\end{align}
By the nonzero-mode Green-tail estimate in (P6), every surviving Fourier component is bounded at large $r$ by an exponential with rate at least $p_{\min}$.  Thus
\begin{align}
\Psi(x,x_3)
&=O\left(e^{-p_{\min}r}\right)\\
&=O\left(\exp\left[-\frac{2\pi r}{NL_3}\right]\right).
\end{align}
Since $\Psi=(g^2/(4\pi iL_4))\vec\sigma$ differs from $\vec\sigma$ by a nonzero constant scalar factor, the same large-distance estimate holds for $\vec\sigma$:
\begin{align}
\vec\sigma(x,x_3)
&=O\left(\exp\left[-\frac{2\pi |x|}{NL_3}\right]\right).
\end{align}
The associated transverse scale is the inverse of the first allowed nonzero momentum,
\begin{align}
p_{\min}^{-1}&=\frac{NL_3}{2\pi}.
\end{align}

\textbf{Final Result.}
\[
\boxed{
\vec\sigma(x,x_3)=O\left(\exp\left[-\frac{2\pi |x|}{NL_3}\right]\right),
\qquad
\text{localization scale}=\frac{NL_3}{2\pi}}
\]
The exponential localization is therefore not an extra assumption: it is the long-distance consequence of the shifted, zero-mode-free $NL_3$-periodic image chain.
\hfill$\blacksquare$

deeper_reasoning_3:
\textbf{Claim.}
The piecewise magnetic flux carried by a shifted $\vec\alpha_n$ monopole is exactly the center-vortex kink condition.  Crossing the monopole position changes the vortex flux by the monopole magnetic charge:
\[
\vec\Phi(x_*+0)-\vec\Phi(x_*-0)=2\pi\vec\alpha_n.
\]

\textbf{Premises.}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{(P1)} & N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge2},\quad 0<x_*<L_3.\\[1mm]
\text{(P2)} & \displaystyle \vec\nu_{r+N}=\vec\nu_r\quad\text{for all integer labels }r.\\[2mm]
\text{(P3)} & \displaystyle \vec\alpha_n=\vec\nu_n-\vec\nu_{n+1}.\\[2mm]
\text{(P4)} & \displaystyle
\vec\Phi(x_3)=
\begin{cases}
2\pi\vec\nu_n, & x_*<x_3<L_3,\\
2\pi\vec\nu_{n+1}, & 0\le x_3<x_*.
\end{cases}\\[6mm]
\text{(P5)} & \displaystyle \text{The shifted boundary condition identifies }x_3=L_3\text{ with }x_3=0
\text{ together with the cyclic label shift.}
\end{array}
\]

\textbf{Derivation.}
Take the right and left limits of (P4) at the monopole position:
\begin{align}
\vec\Phi(x_*+0)&=2\pi\vec\nu_n,\\
\vec\Phi(x_*-0)&=2\pi\vec\nu_{n+1}.
\end{align}
Therefore
\begin{align}
\vec\Phi(x_*+0)-\vec\Phi(x_*-0)
&=2\pi\vec\nu_n-2\pi\vec\nu_{n+1}\\
&=2\pi(\vec\nu_n-\vec\nu_{n+1})\\
&=2\pi\vec\alpha_n
&&\text{by (P3)}.
\end{align}
Thus the monopole is the point where the fundamental vortex flux label changes from $\vec\nu_{n+1}$ to $\vec\nu_n$.

Now check that the fundamental interval boundary does not introduce an additional physical kink.  From (P4),
\begin{align}
\lim_{x_3\to L_3^-}\vec\Phi(x_3)&=2\pi\vec\nu_n,\\
\lim_{x_3\to 0^+}\vec\Phi(x_3)&=2\pi\vec\nu_{n+1}.
\end{align}
By (P5), the shift identification at the boundary cyclically relabels the flux sector.  Hence the apparent change between these two representatives is the imposed twist identification, not a second monopole source in the fundamental cell.  The only intrinsic jump inside $0\le x_3<L_3$ is the jump at $x_*$ computed above.

The sign is also fixed.  Reversing the crossing direction gives
\begin{align}
\vec\Phi(x_*-0)-\vec\Phi(x_*+0)
&=-2\pi\vec\alpha_n,
\end{align}
which is the antimonopole or oppositely oriented kink.  Hence the orientation of the flux jump records the magnetic charge.

\textbf{Final Result.}
\[
\boxed{
\vec\Phi(x_*+0)-\vec\Phi(x_*-0)=2\pi\vec\alpha_n}
\]
together with
\[
\boxed{
\vec\Phi(x_3)=2\pi\vec\nu_n\text{ above the source},\qquad
\vec\Phi(x_3)=2\pi\vec\nu_{n+1}\text{ below the source}}
\]
shows that the shifted monopole is exactly a kink between adjacent fundamental center-vortex flux sectors.
\hfill$\blacksquare$

deeper_reasoning_4:
\textbf{Claim.}
A fundamental Wilson loop detects only the signed $N$-ality of the total enclosed fundamental vortex flux.  If the surface bounded by $C$ is pierced by oriented fundamental fluxes
\[
2\pi\epsilon_j\vec\nu_{a_j},\qquad \epsilon_j\in\{-1,1\},
\]
and
\[
Q:=\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j,
\]
then
\[
W(C)=e^{-2\pi iQ/N}.
\]
In particular, one positively oriented vortex gives $e^{-2\pi i/N}$, one oppositely oriented vortex gives $e^{2\pi i/N}$, and zero total oriented flux gives $1$.

\textbf{Premises.}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{(P1)} & N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge2}.\\[1mm]
\text{(P2)} & \displaystyle \vec\nu_m\cdot\vec\nu_a=\delta_{ma}-\frac{1}{N}
\qquad\text{for }m,a=1,\ldots,N.\\[3mm]
\text{(P3)} & \displaystyle W(C):=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{m=1}^{N}
\exp\left(i\vec\nu_m\cdot\int_C\vec a\right).\\[3mm]
\text{(P4)} & \displaystyle \int_C\vec a=2\pi\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j\vec\nu_{a_j},
\qquad \epsilon_j\in\{-1,1\}.\\[3mm]
\text{(P5)} & \displaystyle e^{2\pi i s}=1\quad\text{for every }s\in\mathbb{Z}.\\[2mm]
\text{(P6)} & \displaystyle Q:=\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j.
\end{array}
\]

\textbf{Derivation.}
For a fixed weight label $m$, define the signed count
\begin{align}
s_m&:=\sum_{\{j:\,a_j=m\}}\epsilon_j .
\end{align}
Then $s_m\in\mathbb{Z}$.  Using (P2),
\begin{align}
\vec\nu_m\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j\vec\nu_{a_j}
&=\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j\left(\delta_{m a_j}-\frac{1}{N}\right)\\
&=s_m-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j\\
&=s_m-\frac{Q}{N}.
\end{align}
Substitute the total flux into the Wilson loop:
\begin{align}
W(C)
&=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{m=1}^{N}
\exp\left(2\pi i\vec\nu_m\cdot\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j\vec\nu_{a_j}\right)
&&\text{by (P3) and (P4)}\\
&=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{m=1}^{N}
\exp\left(2\pi i\left(s_m-\frac{Q}{N}\right)\right)\\
&=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{m=1}^{N}
e^{2\pi i s_m}e^{-2\pi iQ/N}\\
&=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{m=1}^{N}
e^{-2\pi iQ/N}
&&\text{by (P5)}\\
&=e^{-2\pi iQ/N}.
\end{align}
The result is independent of the individual labels $a_j$ and depends only on the signed total number $Q$ modulo $N$.

For $Q=1$,
\begin{align}
W(C)&=e^{-2\pi i/N}.
\end{align}
For $Q=0$,
\begin{align}
W(C)&=1.
\end{align}
For $Q=-1$,
\begin{align}
W(C)&=e^{2\pi i/N}.
\end{align}

\textbf{Final Result.}
\[
\boxed{W(C)=e^{-2\pi iQ/N},\qquad Q=\sum_{j=1}^{r}\epsilon_j}
\]
so the fundamental loop detects the enclosed oriented center-vortex number modulo $N$, exactly as required for the center element of an $SU(N)$ vortex.
\hfill$\blacksquare$

deeper_reasoning_5:
\textbf{Claim.}
The root-of-unity constraint in the two-dimensional vortex gas is the same $\mathbb{Z}_N$ center-neutrality condition detected by the fundamental Wilson loop.  If a gas configuration contains $r$ vortices and $\bar r$ antivortices, define its signed vortex number
\[
Q:=r-\bar r .
\]
Then
\[
\Delta_N(Q)=N
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
Q\in N\mathbb{Z}
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
e^{-2\pi iQ/N}=1,
\]
and otherwise $\Delta_N(Q)=0$.

\textbf{Premises.}
\[
\begin{array}{ll}
\text{(P1)} & N\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge2},\quad Q\in\mathbb{Z}.\\[1mm]
\text{(P2)} & \displaystyle \Delta_N(Q):=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\exp\left(\frac{2\pi ikQ}{N}\right).\\[3mm]
\text{(P3)} & \displaystyle \Delta_N(Q)=
\begin{cases}
N, & Q\in N\mathbb{Z},\\
0, & Q\notin N\mathbb{Z}.
\end{cases}\\[5mm]
\text{(P4)} & \displaystyle \text{A configuration with signed vortex number }Q
\text{ gives fundamental Wilson phase }W_Q=e^{-2\pi iQ/N}.\\[2mm]
\text{(P5)} & \displaystyle e^{-2\pi iQ/N}=1\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad Q\in N\mathbb{Z}.
\end{array}
\]

\textbf{Derivation.}
By (P3), the projector keeps exactly those configurations whose signed vortex number satisfies
\begin{align}
Q&\in N\mathbb{Z}.
\end{align}
By (P5), this condition is equivalent to
\begin{align}
e^{-2\pi iQ/N}&=1.
\end{align}
Using the Wilson-loop response in (P4), the same condition is
\begin{align}
W_Q&=1.
\end{align}
Therefore the occupation-number constraint imposed in the vortex gas is not a separate algebraic device; it is precisely the condition that the total oriented center flux of the whole gas is invisible to a fundamental loop at infinity.

Equivalently,
\begin{align}
\Delta_N(Q)
&=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\exp\left(\frac{2\pi ikQ}{N}\right)\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{N}\left(e^{-2\pi iQ/N}\right)^{-k}\\
&=\sum_{k=1}^{N}W_Q^{-k}.
\end{align}
If $W_Q=1$, every term equals $1$ and $\Delta_N(Q)=N$.  If $W_Q\ne1$, the sum over all $N$ powers of a nontrivial $N$th root of unity vanishes, so $\Delta_N(Q)=0$.

\textbf{Final Result.}
\[
\boxed{
\Delta_N(Q)\text{ projects onto exactly the configurations with trivial total center phase }W_Q=1.}
\]
Thus the branch projection in the partition-function derivation and the Wilson-loop center response in the microscopic vortex test are two expressions of the same $\mathbb{Z}_N$ selection rule.
\hfill$\blacksquare$
